[{:seon.task/name "a-deftype-method-call-takes-a-site-cache"
  :seon.task/plan "Correction 2026-11-11: measured in isolation on both today's pin and the Jolt at upstream's head, a protocol method call on a deftype costs 10-13 ticks and allocates NOTHING where the site sees one type; the allocation this task was filed for was seen inside the sorted set through Seon and may be another call shape (a host method call, about 190 ticks; or a call of five or more arguments, 80 B plus 16 B an argument): see task jolt.host/a-host-method-call-costs-a-dispatch. Do not build a site cache on this entry's evidence; name the shape first. Earlier text follows. Found 2026-10-27: an interop call of a deftype's declared method, (.lookup s k cmp), (.cons s k cmp), (.root s) on persistent-sorted-set's PersistentSortedSet, costs 60 ticks and 80 B with no work in it, and a PSS lookup with a free comparator ~300 B. Cause: jolt-host-site-call0..3 (reference-code/jolt/host/chez/java/host-static.ss) cache only a jhost receiver; a jrec builds the argument vector, walks method-dispatch-arms (record-method-dispatch) and the dotform arm's cond (dot-forms.ss), whose rest args become a list, before jrec-method-arity finds the method. The fix: the site keeps (desc . method) for a jrec receiver, as jrec-field-site-ref does for a field read, filled only when the dotform arm would itself take the declared method (no user-override arm, not a field read, not a class field with no args, not an IFn or transient method the arm answers first), and calls it with no vector. Proofs: a Jolt chez test of bytes a call (0 past the method's own), and seon.db-test/a-value-costs-its-index-inserts's overrun closing: that test is green on main because it carries `:overrun {:task \"jolt.backend/a-deftype-method-call-takes-a-site-cache\" ...}`, so green says nothing until that key is deleted."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.db-test/a-value-costs-its-index-inserts}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-try-entered-allocates-nothing"
  :seon.task/plan "Found 2026-10-27: entering a try/catch whose body does not throw costs 176 B on Jolt ((try (compare a b) (catch Exception e 0)) against 0 B for the compare alone), so a library that wraps a hot call in a try pays it per call; Datahike's compare-value and cmp-attr-nil each grew a type test to skip the try (jolt/compare-value-no-try, jolt/attribute-compare-without-try). The try is emitted at its site as a one-shot escape and a hand-pushed handler (Jolt c22cb9c0, 00a3b321, reference-code/jolt/jolt-core/jolt/backend_scheme.clj); find what of that allocates per entry (the escape continuation, the handler frame, a closure over the body) and make the no-throw path allocate nothing. Once it does, Datahike's two type tests can go. Proof: a Jolt chez test of bytes per try entered (0), and seon.db-test/an-identity-costs-its-seek."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{jolt.backend-test/a-try-that-does-not-throw-allocates-nothing}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-var-resolved-by-several-fibers-at-once-is-safe"
  :seon.task/plan "Contained 2026-10-27 (jolt 8e5f6304), cause not found. Datahike's background gc hung one run in ten on Jolt: a fiber threw 'car: false is not a pair' and its result channel never answered. The Chez inspector's frames of the failing continuation, innermost first: jolt-stack-check! <- $call-uncounted <- string-hash <- do-hash <- $gen-hashtable-ref <- jolt-var <- var-deref <- do-alts* <- async/merge. So the raise is in the stack-limit hook (rt.ss jolt-stack-check!, installed by sa-gc-install-after-rendezvous!, run after every collection on every thread that took part), which var-deref's allocation only triggered; the earlier trace named var-deref and string-equal? because its frames were abbreviated. Interleaved on one build, 40 rounds each: 5 failures with the hook, 0 with JOLT_STACK_LIMIT=-1. Not the var table (a Chez stress with a locked writer forcing resizes and 7 to 9 unlocked readers, and the same shape in the runtime with 6 threads and 6 fibers, found no error and no lost entry), not short-lived threads starting during collections, and not sa-current-stack-bytes alone (copied into Chez, forced collections at random depths: none). The failure depends on the build (110 runs of builds with debug output: 0; builds without: about 1 in 10) and vanished when the hook's three calls were wrapped, so the faulting car is probably inlined into the hook and layout-dependent; the only unchecked car there is the weak memo's (sa-current-stack-bytes). Done: a failed measurement no longer raises into the computation (it is 'cannot measure', its contract) and is kept, with its frames, for jolt.host/stack-check-failure; proof test/chez/stack-limit-test.ss section 5. Open: read what the kept record says once a run has one (the verification repro, scripts in the root session's notes: bg2.clj prints it), then fix the faulting expression and let the hook's own failure be an error again, as a typed one answered where it lands."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{jolt.backend-test/forty-interleaved-rounds-of-background-gc-all-end}}
 {:seon.task/name "an-unchanged-namespace-is-not-emitted-again"
  :seon.task/plan "Not ready: the owner or the Optimization session must first say, from the count of builds a day, whether to build this per-form emit cache or jolt.backend/whole-program-inference-costs-what-changed (the plan states that count decides between them; an independent review of the cache key is also required before merge). test-audit item A (2026-10-27, section 5): a one-comment edit to src/seon/profile.clj cost a 48.6 s bin/build seon. Measured (JOLT_BUILD_PROFILE=1, seon.main, 313 units): an unchanged jolt build is 41 s and Chez compiles 1 of 313 units (the per-unit .so cache works, host/chez/build.ss bld-compile-app-units!); the time is the front end over the whole program every build: whole-program inference 11.1 s (fixpoint 8.2 s), emit of every namespace 20.1 s (run-passes 8.7 s, emit-top 8.7 s, dce-refs 2.0 s), write flat.ss 3.8 s (2.0-2.3 s since jolt 6c4f23a0, units written once from memory). Saves cycles, not only wall time (root, 2026-10-27: cycles are the owner's complaint). Before starting: how many builds a day run once the test fixes merge (test-audit's tracking table) decides between this and jolt.backend/whole-program-inference-costs-what-changed. What emit reads and writes (research, 2026-10-27, paths in reference-code/jolt): per namespace, emit (host/chez/emit-image.ss ei-emit-ns-records) takes the IR the whole-program walk made (ei-next-cached; macros already expanded there, build.ss bld-wp-infer!) and reads the whole loaded program: var cells and their final roots (rt.ss var-cell-lookup, var-redefined?; host-contract.ss hc-resolve-cell, hc-var-root-callable?, hc-var-dynamic?, hc-var-linked?), whole-program facts on the unit (jolt-core/jolt/passes/types.clj param-seeds-for, param-num-seeds-for, :field-types, :pm-rets, clone-double-ret?; backend_scheme.clj clone-site?), record shapes and protocol maps (records.ss chez-record-shapes-map, chez-protocol-methods-map), the inline stash (host-contract.ss hc-inline-ir) and the direct-link sets (backend_scheme.clj direct-linkable?, direct-link-fn?). It writes three tables later namespaces and the DCE read: the direct-link sets (backend_scheme.clj emit-def-cached), the inline stash (hc-stash-inline!, IR values) and the spliced set (hc-mark-spliced!, read by dce.ss). The sound design: a per-form cache keyed on a hash of the form's IR (which carries its macro expansion: an expander runs arbitrary code, so its inputs cannot be keyed) and the mode flags, holding the read set recorded at those chokepoints as (fact, digest of its value); a lookup re-reads each recorded fact and hits only when every digest matches (Bazel's and Datahike's read-range validation); a hit replays the three writes, which needs a serializer for IR values (the inline stash), Jolt's first. The name counters (build.ss bld-seed-name-counters!) give each namespace hash(ns) & 0x7FFFF with linear probing in first-call order, so two colliding namespaces name things in build order: the assigned slot goes in the key, or slots are made collision-free first. Estimate: several days; 41 s -> about 17 s on a change that moves no other form's facts. Proofs: the whole program emitted cached and uncached is byte-for-byte equal; a change to a referenced var's facts re-emits its referrers; a cost proof that a one-file change's build is flat across program sizes. An independent review of the key before it merges (root's condition). Not this: JOLT_NO_WP_INFER for dev builds (the binary would allocate unlike main's, and byte cost proofs read it). css stays keyed on src/ (Tailwind scans it for classes). Merged from jolt.backend/emit-runs-in-parallel-processes (2026-11-10 triage): gains the lower-priority alternative for the same 20 s emit (compute direct-link and inline facts before emit, fork children per slice) and its proof that parallel-emitted records equal sequential ones byte for byte; the parallel emit needs the same facts-before-emit split."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{}}
 {:seon.task/name "whole-program-inference-costs-what-changed"
  :seon.task/plan "Not ready: needs jolt.backend/an-unchanged-namespace-is-not-emitted-again and the same builds-a-day decision (Optimization session / the owner) on which of the two to build first. After jolt.backend/an-unchanged-namespace-is-not-emitted-again, a one-file build is still about 17 s, 11.1 s of it whole-program inference (seon.main, 2026-10-27: wp fixpoint 8.2 s, wp analyze 2.1 s, contagion prepass 0.2 s; host/chez/build.ss bld-wp-infer!). It analyzes every namespace and iterates a global fixpoint from nothing on every build. Correct: cache each namespace's analysis keyed on its text (the emit cache's key discipline: what it reads, recorded), and start the fixpoint from the last build's result, so facts no change reaches converge in one pass and a change re-walks what its facts reach. Estimate about 17 s -> 5 s. Proofs: the fixpoint's result from the last result equals the result from nothing, over the whole program, for a sample of one-namespace edits (a body, a signature, a type hint); a cost proof that inference after a one-file change costs what the change reaches."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/needs #{"jolt.backend/an-unchanged-namespace-is-not-emitted-again"}
  :seon.task/proofs #{}}
 {:seon.task/name "an-emitted-unit-names-nothing-from-the-jolt-binary"
  :seon.task/plan "Confirmed 2026-10-27 (jolt-compare, building seon.main): any change to Jolt's own image moves the gensym numbers of a few syntax-quote templates in the emitted app units, 18 of 313 (rewrite-clj.node.*, datahike.query.execute, clojure.test.check.random: node__25__auto becomes node__126__auto, and the units' marker offsets with them). Two builds from one Jolt binary are byte-identical, a clean rebuild of the same pin reproduces the old bytes, and a build from a Jolt that differs only in a comment of an embedded runtime file shifts the same 18. Likely cause: those templates are read during the build's load of the app from source (host/chez/build.ss, before the whole-program walk), where the reader's syntax-quote counter is not seeded per namespace (bld-seed-name-counters! seeds the wp and emit phases), so their numbers depend on what the Jolt binary read before. Cost today: none for the unit cache (its key carries the runtime fingerprint, which any Jolt change moves), but a unit's text is not a function of the app and the compiler's version alone, which the emit cache (jolt.backend/an-unchanged-namespace-is-not-emitted-again) and any cross-build comparison need. Correct: seed the reader's counters per namespace for the source load too, or re-read macro templates in the seeded phase. Proof: two Jolt binaries differing only in an embedded comment emit byte-identical units for seon.main."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{jolt.backend-test/two-jolt-binaries-differing-only-embedded-comment-emit}}
 {:seon.task/name "eval-and-the-loader-compile-every-form-alike"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner: deps (tests-4 asked, 2026-11-15: the class is Jolt's, so a Jolt gate run once per pin, every caller gains). PRIOR WORK [searched Jolt's Makefile gates for eval/loader/parity]: no gate compares the two paths. Nearest: evalbydigest (Makefile:852: the same forms in a second process load by digest), corpus (runs a corpus through the loader). The class: eval compiles a form that a reader or data reader has already turned into values, while the file loader compiles reader forms, and emit-quoted had value branches only for collections. A quoted #uuid broke every test agent's load 482 times (fixed by gate emitquotedvalues, Jolt 6b77996b); any other place the two paths differ is the same class. Inside Seon, evaluating every stored form cost 1.2B ticks and 13 GB for 5 test files [M, tests-4]: too heavy for a test. Design: a Jolt gate reads each form of the corpus and stdlib files twice, as reader forms (the loader's input) and as values (read-string, eval's input), compiles both, and requires equal results or the same typed error. COST: n = the corpus's forms (~thousands); O(n) compiles, about twice the corpus gate's work; lower bound one compile per form per path. Proof: the gate, red first on a commit before 6b77996b (the #uuid case). Where Jolt's gates and fork rules live: docs/forks.md#jolt."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{jolt.backend-test/eval-and-the-loader-compile-every-form-alike}
  :seon.task/estimate #:jolt.backend{emit-quoted {:n 5000, :order :linear, :ticks 50000000, :bytes 500000000}}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-cfn-outside-a-def-resolves-once"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner: deps. Root's ask (runtime's 759M-tick root step, half its samples in dlsym under S_foreign_entry, runs/stop-overran-87484/sample-87484.txt). Read: the backend keeps a cfn's resolved procedure in a cell of the enclosing def's scope (reference-code/jolt/jolt-core/jolt/backend_scheme.clj:2445-2460, *cache-cells*); outside a def it emits (let ((p #f)) lambda) at the site, and Chez's lookup caches no dynamic symbol (reference-code/chez/c/foreign.c:159: every handle dlsym'd again each time). Measured on Jolt 6b77996b [/tmp/deps-siginfo/cfn.clj, cfn2.clj]: ns a call, defcfn 5, cfn in a defn 9, reify 2,141, defrecord 2,460, deftype 2,400, letfn 2,175. Fix the class: every cfn site gets one cell in its top-level compilation unit, whatever form encloses it, so a site resolves once a process (a reload of the unit resolves once again). Estimate: one cell read a call (~5 ns, 0 bytes), against ~2,200 ns and two dlsym walks today. Sibling bug found beside it: a cfn in a zero-argument defmethod throws 'cdr: ... is not a pair' (cfn2.clj): a gate of its own in the same landing. Not yet tied to root's step: no Seon or library cfn sits in a method; runtime-2's stop capture names the step. Proof, red first: a cfn in reify, deftype and letfn costs within 2x of defcfn's per call. See docs/forks.md#jolt."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{jolt.backend-test/a-cfn-in-a-method-resolves-its-symbol-once}
  :seon.task/estimate #:jolt.backend{emit-ffi-fn {:n 1, :order :constant, :ticks 10, :bytes 0}}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-redefined-var-is-what-compiled-library-code-calls", :seon.task/kind :work, :seon.task/proofs #{}, :seon.task/plan "Owner: deps. Found by data, 2026-11-15 18:25 [M]: on the pin ba387e82ff (Jolt c28efb7a), with-redefs of konserve.core/get-immutable reaches a call from evaluated code but not datahike's compiled calls: datahike.index.persistent-set/mark over a 200-datom store marked 64 keys, every one read through k/get-immutable, and the redef counted 0 (result/d4c91e8a; the var's value was the same fn, so the call skipped the var). seon.db-test/a-collections-mark-reads-each-node-once was green at 17:14 and red at 17:30 at the same digest 3ed9db1450e23b51 (target/results), across the pin: '0 reads for 117 nodes'. The class: every test that counts or stubs a library function by with-redefs (alter-var-root) now sees nothing from compiled library code, silently: seon.db-test's store-opens test (konserve.impl.defaults/deprecated!, asserts nothing was seen) passes whatever happens. Read first: what in the pin's Jolt (929e0b80..c28efb7a, or the Chez side) changed how a compiled call to another namespace's defn resolves (a direct link, an inlined or cached var value). Fix at the cause in Jolt, so a var's root is what every call reads (Clojure's default without direct linking), or say where direct linking is meant and with the owner's yes; never a test-side workaround. CORRECTED (deps, 2026-11-15 18:30): not the pin. Jolt 62476381..c28efb7a touches no jolt-core file; Chez's range is C-only. The 17:14 green was bin/test on tree 99e1d87d (main plus the pin); every red (17:30, 18:09, 18:10) was written by an agent on :main/tests forked from root's image, as was data's root measurement: same pin, same digest, green in a bin/test process and red in the image. [H, deps] the binary image compiles library code with direct calls between its own known procedures; a bin/test process calls through the var. In-system test runs began today, so this was exposed, not caused, today. Owner: deps; next measurement one call site of psi/mark in the image against a cold load."}]
